### What is horizontal scaling and why must the service be stateless?

Horizontal scaling is the foundation for handling traffic surges. It works only if the service is stateless, meaning no per-user session is stored in local memory. Any instance can handle any request, which means you can run ten or a hundred identical instances behind a load balancer. Session or user state goes into a shared store like Redis.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.data.redis.connection.RedisConnectionFactory;
import org.springframework.session.data.redis.config.annotation.web.http.EnableRedisHttpSession;

@Configuration
@EnableRedisHttpSession(maxInactiveIntervalInSeconds = 1800)
public class SessionConfig {
    // Sessions stored in Redis, not in JVM heap.
    // Any instance can serve any request.
}
```

```text
Stateless service behind load balancer:
  Request 1 -> Instance A (reads session from Redis)
  Request 2 -> Instance C (reads same session from Redis)
  Request 3 -> Instance B (reads same session from Redis)
  All three see the same session data.
```

### How does auto-scaling work?

Auto-scaling makes instance addition automatic. The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) watches a signal like CPU utilization or requests per second and increases the replica count when the signal crosses a target. For bursty, queue-driven workloads, KEDA scales on queue depth, which reacts faster than CPU for spiky loads.

```yaml
# Kubernetes HPA configuration
apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: account-service-hpa
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: account-service
  minReplicas: 3
  maxReplicas: 30
  metrics:
    - type: Resource
      resource:
        name: cpu
        target:
          type: Utilization
          averageUtilization: 70
    - type: Pods
      pods:
        metric:
          name: http_requests_per_second
        target:
          type: AverageValue
          averageValue: "100"
```

```text
Normal traffic:   3 pods, CPU at 30%
Salary-day surge: CPU hits 70% -> HPA scales to 15 pods in 2 minutes
Surge passes:     CPU drops -> HPA scales back to 3 pods over 10 minutes
```

### Why is caching the single biggest lever?

Caching removes load from the database, which is usually the first tier to buckle. With a cache-aside strategy the service checks Redis first, and only on a miss does it hit the database and then populate the cache with a short time-to-live (TTL). A thousand balance checks per second mostly hit Redis in microseconds instead of hammering PostgreSQL.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.cache.annotation.Cacheable;
import org.springframework.cache.annotation.CacheEvict;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class AccountService {

    private final AccountRepository repository;

    public AccountService(AccountRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    @Cacheable(value = "balances", key = "#accountId")
    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        // Cache miss: reads from PostgreSQL
        return repository.findBalanceByAccountId(accountId);
    }

    @CacheEvict(value = "balances", key = "#accountId")
    public void updateBalance(String accountId, BigDecimal newBalance) {
        repository.updateBalance(accountId, newBalance);
        // Cache evicted: next read will refresh from DB
    }
}
```

```yaml
# Redis cache configuration
spring:
  cache:
    type: redis
    redis:
      time-to-live: 30s
      cache-null-values: false
```

```text
Without cache: 1000 req/s * DB query = 1000 DB queries/s
With cache:    1000 req/s * 95% hit rate = 50 DB queries/s
Result: 20x reduction in database load during surge
```

### How does asynchronous processing keep latency low?

Async processing moves slow work off the request path. Instead of doing everything synchronously while the customer waits, the service does the minimal critical step and drops a message onto Kafka for the rest. A transfer updates balances synchronously but publishes an event so that statement generation, notifications, and analytics happen later.

```java
package com.bank.transfer;

import org.springframework.kafka.core.KafkaTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, TransferEvent> kafka;

    public TransferService(AccountRepository accountRepo,
                            KafkaTemplate<String, TransferEvent> kafka) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
        this.kafka = kafka;
    }

    @Transactional
    public TransferResult transfer(String from, String to,
                                    BigDecimal amount) {
        // Critical path: update balances synchronously
        accountRepo.debit(from, amount);
        accountRepo.credit(to, amount);

        // Non-critical path: publish event for async consumers
        kafka.send("transfer-events",
            new TransferEvent(from, to, amount));

        return TransferResult.success("Transfer completed");
    }
}
```

```text
Sync-only flow:  debit + credit + statement + notification + analytics = 800ms
Async flow:      debit + credit = 50ms  (rest happens via Kafka consumers later)
Result: 16x latency improvement on the request path
```

### How do you scale the database?

The database is usually the first tier to buckle under surge. The main techniques are connection pooling, read replicas, and sharding.

```yaml
# HikariCP connection pool configuration
spring:
  datasource:
    hikari:
      maximum-pool-size: 20
      minimum-idle: 5
      connection-timeout: 2000
      idle-timeout: 300000
      max-lifetime: 1800000
```

```java
package com.bank.config;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.jdbc.datasource.lookup.AbstractRoutingDataSource;
import javax.sql.DataSource;
import java.util.Map;

@Configuration
public class ReadReplicaConfig {

    @Bean
    public DataSource routingDataSource(DataSource primary,
                                         DataSource replica) {
        AbstractRoutingDataSource routing = new AbstractRoutingDataSource() {
            @Override
            protected Object determineCurrentLookupKey() {
                return TransactionSynchronizationManager
                    .isCurrentTransactionReadOnly()
                    ? "replica" : "primary";
            }
        };
        routing.setTargetDataSources(Map.of(
            "primary", primary,
            "replica", replica
        ));
        routing.setDefaultTargetDataSource(primary);
        return routing;
    }
}
```

```text
Database scaling techniques:
  Connection pool:  prevents opening a new connection per request
  Read replicas:    route heavy reads to copies, writes to primary
  Sharding:         split data across DBs by account_id for write scale
```

### What is rate limiting and load shedding?

Rate limiting and load shedding protect the system when it is genuinely beyond capacity. The gateway rejects or throttles the least important traffic (for example, defer analytics calls) to protect the core path like transfers and balance checks.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.ratelimit.KeyResolver;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

@Configuration
public class RateLimitConfig {

    @Bean
    public KeyResolver userKeyResolver() {
        return exchange -> Mono.just(
            exchange.getRequest().getHeaders()
                .getFirst("X-Client-Id")
        );
    }
}
```

```yaml
# Gateway rate limiting with Redis
spring:
  cloud:
    gateway:
      routes:
        - id: account-service
          uri: lb://account-service
          filters:
            - name: RequestRateLimiter
              args:
                redis-rate-limiter.replenishRate: 100
                redis-rate-limiter.burstCapacity: 200
                redis-rate-limiter.requestedTokens: 1
                key-resolver: "#{@userKeyResolver}"
```

```text
Rate limit response:
  HTTP 429 Too Many Requests
  Retry-After: 5
  X-RateLimit-Remaining: 0
```

### How do you monitor and respond to a surge?

Watch latency percentiles, error rates, queue depth, and saturation. Set up dashboards and alerts so the team can respond before the system breaks.

```java
package com.bank.monitoring;

import io.micrometer.core.instrument.Counter;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.Timer;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class TransferMetrics {

    private final Timer transferTimer;
    private final Counter transferCounter;
    private final Counter failureCounter;

    public TransferMetrics(MeterRegistry registry) {
        this.transferTimer = Timer.builder("transfer.duration")
            .description("Transfer processing time")
            .register(registry);
        this.transferCounter = Counter.builder("transfer.total")
            .description("Total transfers")
            .register(registry);
        this.failureCounter = Counter.builder("transfer.failures")
            .description("Failed transfers")
            .register(registry);
    }

    public Timer getTransferTimer() { return transferTimer; }
    public Counter getTransferCounter() { return transferCounter; }
    public Counter getFailureCounter() { return failureCounter; }
}
```

```text
Key Prometheus alerts:
  transfer_duration_seconds{quantile="0.99"} > 2   -> P99 latency alert
  transfer_failures_total rate > 0.05               -> Error rate alert
  hikaricp_connections_active == hikaricp_connections_max -> Pool exhaustion
  kafka_consumer_lag > 10000                         -> Consumer falling behind
```

### What is the step-by-step principle for handling surges?

The guiding principle is: start simple, measure, find the current bottleneck, relieve exactly that tier, and repeat. Never scale everything speculatively.

| Stage | Bottleneck | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Slow DB queries | Add indexes, optimize queries |
| 2 | Single app instance | Stateless app + load balancer |
| 3 | DB read load | Cache (Redis) + read replicas + CDN |
| 4 | Sync slow work | Async queue (Kafka) + rate limiting |
| 5 | Single DB write | Shard by account_id |
| 6 | Manual scaling | Auto-scaling (HPA/KEDA) + multi-region |
